Our evaluation has several limitations that future work should address.

\paragraph{Model scale.}
All experiments use Qwen3-0.6B; batch-level statistics ($\aeff$, ESS) may behave differently at larger scales where batch sizes are typically larger, potentially shifting the balance between quality-binding and stability-binding regimes.

\paragraph{Controlled contamination.}
We inject synthetic text via uniform random mixing at a fixed ratio ($\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$).
Real web contamination is domain-skewed and temporally non-stationary; the method's effectiveness under such conditions remains untested.

\paragraph{Single detector family.}
We evaluate only a TF-IDF + Logistic Regression detector.
The observation that a lightweight detector suffices is based on a single comparison point and does not establish a causal relationship between calibration quality and reweighting effectiveness.

\paragraph{Effect size.}
Method-level PPL differences (${\sim}0.04$ at $\rho{=}0.4$) are small in absolute terms, arising in a regime where the pretrained checkpoint (PPL 22.97) has already been optimized by $5{+}$ PPL points through continued pretraining.
Whether these differences amplify at higher contamination rates or larger scales is an empirical question.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter positioning.}
While $\tau$ and $\nmin$ have principled defaults and a documented safe range, their theoretical derivation remains open: the convergence analysis motivating $\tau$ \citep{wang2026survive} assumes known contamination proportions, which we do not.

